  "text": "**Solution 1 is the better of the two, and its answer, d_c = 8, is plausible but not verified.**\n\nI checked Solution 1's setup. Its transition rates are consistent with a common normalization (every rate is half the unscaled edge-clock rate). The radial generator on k ≥ 2 gives the recurrence (d−1)z² − (d−s)z + 1 = 0, whose continuum edge is s₀ = d − 2√(d−1), matching the random walk rate. The boundary conditions at D = 0 and D = 1 give (d−1)z = (d−1) − s − s/(d−2s). With these, z = (d−2s)/(d−s) is consistent. At d = 9, s = 3 satisfies both equations, and 3 < 9 − 4√2 ≈ 3.34, so a discrete eigenvalue exists there. The discriminant (d−1)(d−9) then places the threshold between d = 8 and d = 9.\n\nTwo caveats weaken the result:\n- The distance process is transient for d ≥ 3, so the relevant Hilbert space is weighted by the reversing measure (d−1)^k. The eigenfunction z^k is in that space only if z < 1/√(d−1). At d = 9 the root z = 1/2 fails this, so the bound-state argument needs more justification than Solution 1 gives.\n- I could not confirm the threshold against the literature.\n\nSolution 2 offers no derivation for d_c = 3. Its \"noise cascade\" mechanism is asserted rather than computed, so its answer is unsupported.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
